o attach a single or a pair. The choice is between three plays: a triplet and a single, a triplet and a pair, or playing a pair straight.\n\nPlaying the triplet with a single makes the pair straight more feasible in future turns, but it means I will keep the 77 pair which might be a small burden later on. Playing the triplet with a pair dumps the low pairs, but is it a good move overall?\n\nMy key strategy is to manage my 10s and clear my hand with an attached pair or playing straights.\n\nPlaying the 9-9-10-10-J-J pair straight, however, seems like a weak choice since it uses multiple cards and gets beaten. I need to make a play with a high rank and high value.\n\nGiven all these assessments, I think the best move is to play the full house (10-10-10-7-7). It utilizes the best elements of my hand structure. Playing this clears 5 cards and removes a low pair from my hand, leaving me with pairs and control cards for later. This seems the most logical strategy.\n\nI have decided to dump the 77 with 10-10-10, and keep all other cards for a better strategic move. This clears a small pair which would be a burden in the next turns.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The hand contains a strong triplet structure (10-10-10) and several pairs (77, 99, JJ). Playing a \'Full House\' (Triplet with Pair) allows for the efficient removal of cards, specifically clearing the low pair of 7s which would otherwise be a liability. While a straight from 6 to Q is possible, it would break all existing pairs and the triplet, leaving a very disjointed hand of singles (7, 9, 10, 10, J) which is structurally poor. By playing the 10-10-10-7-7, I reduce my hand count significantly while preserving the pairs of 99 and JJ, and keeping the 2 and Red Joker as control cards to regain the lead. This move maximizes structural integrity and plays to the hand\'s strength (pairs and triples) rather than forcing a weak straight.\n\nReason: Playing the triplet of 10s with the pair of 7s (Full House) is the most efficient move. It clears 5 cards at once and disposes of a small pair (77) that is hard to play individually. It preserves the remaining pairs (99, JJ) and high control cards (2, Red Joker), whereas playing a straight would break the hand into weak singles.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥7", "♦7", "♠10", "♥10", "♦10"]}'}
